# air-cargo

## Project Overview
Air cargo flight tracking and trade flow inference system.
Polls ADS-B data from OpenSky Network to track cargo aircraft,
detect anomalous behaviour, and infer freight trade flows.

## Repository Structure
air-cargo/
├── adsb-collector/    OpenSky REST polling → Kafka
├── adsb-analyser/     Flink + SedonaFlink, detection + inference
├── common/            Shared models, constants, enums
├── reference-data/    Airport, operator reference data scripts
└── k8s/               K3s deployment manifests

## Language and Build
- Java 17
- Maven, no parent pom — each module independent
- Do not suggest Gradle, Kotlin, or Spring Boot

## Core Technology Stack
- Apache Flink — stream processing
- Apache SedonaFlink — spatial operations on Flink streams
- Redpanda — Kafka-compatible broker (refer to as Kafka in code)
- PostgreSQL + PostGIS — spatial reference layers + JSONB state
- OkHttp — HTTP client for OpenSky polling
- Jackson — JSON serialisation
- Protobuf — internal Kafka event topics only

## OpenSky Network API
Base URL: https://opensky-network.org/api
Authentication: OAuth2 client credentials
  - client_id and client_secret from environment variables
  - Bearer token, expires every 30 minutes
  - TokenManager class handles automatic refresh

Key endpoints:
  GET /states/all
    - current state vectors, or up to 1hr past (authenticated)
    - optional: time (unix), icao24, bounding box, extended=1
    - filter by bounding box: lamin, lomin, lamax, lomax
    - returns JSON with time + states array

  GET /flights/aircraft
    - flights for specific icao24, max 2 day window
    - updated nightly — previous day or earlier only

  GET /flights/arrival
  GET /flights/departure
    - by airport ICAO code, max 2 day window

Polling interval: every 10 seconds for /states/all
Token refresh: proactively before 30 minute expiry
On startup catch-up: query /states/all with time=now-3600

## State Vector Field Indices
OpenSky returns states as arrays — deserialise by index:
  0:  icao24          hex string, unique transponder address
  1:  callsign        8 chars, nullable
  2:  origin_country  inferred from icao24
  3:  time_position   unix timestamp last position, nullable
  4:  last_contact    unix timestamp last contact
  5:  longitude       WGS-84 decimal degrees, nullable
  6:  latitude        WGS-84 decimal degrees, nullable
  7:  baro_altitude   metres, nullable
  8:  on_ground       boolean
  9:  velocity        m/s over ground, nullable
  10: true_track      degrees clockwise from north, nullable
  11: vertical_rate   m/s positive=climbing, nullable
  12: sensors         receiver IDs, nullable
  13: geo_altitude    metres, nullable
  14: squawk          transponder code, nullable
  15: spi             special purpose indicator boolean
  16: position_source 0=ADS-B 1=ASTERIX 2=MLAT 3=FLARM
  17: category        only present when extended=1

## Aircraft Categories (extended=1)
  4: Large (75,000-300,000 lbs)
  5: High Vortex Large (B-757 class)
  6: Heavy (> 300,000 lbs) — primary interest

## Kafka Topics
  adsb.states          JSON, raw from OpenSky
  events.anomalies     Protobuf
  events.watchlist     Protobuf
  events.trade_flows   Protobuf

## Serialisation
- adsb.states: JSON only — preserve raw OpenSky data
- Internal event topics: Protobuf
- Document state: PostgreSQL JSONB
- Never transcode raw OpenSky data to Protobuf

## Key Identifiers
ICAO24: permanent aircraft identifier, primary key throughout
Callsign: changes per flight, not reliable as identifier
Registration: tail number, links to external registries

## adsb-analyser — Flink Processing
- StreamExecutionEnvironment with SedonaFlink registered
- Always use event time from OpenSky timestamps
- Never use processing time
- RocksDB state backend
- Watermark lag: ~60 seconds

## Two-Tier State
Hot (RocksDB):
  TTL 48-72 hours no contact
  Watchlisted aircraft: TTL suppressed
  On TTL expiry: write snapshot to PostgreSQL

Cold (PostgreSQL JSONB):
  Long-term history, rehydrate into RocksDB on reappearance

## PostgreSQL JSONB Collections
  aircraft        per-aircraft state and history
  watchlist       tracked aircraft + uncertainty state
  trade_flows     inferred cargo movement events
  anomaly_history scored events

## PostGIS Reference Layers
  airports            point + polygon, ICAO codes
  cargo_terminals     cargo ramp areas within airports
  airspace_boundaries FIR boundaries, restricted zones

## Spatial Operations
- ST_DistanceSphere for distance calculations
- Coordinates WGS-84 (EPSG:4326) throughout
- OpenSky returns longitude first, then latitude
  be explicit when constructing Sedona geometry points
- Broadcast state pattern for airport/airspace lookups

## Anomaly Scoring
- Fuzzy 0.0-1.0 confidence scores, not binary thresholds
- Scores accumulate in per-aircraft Flink state
- Alert only when score sustained above threshold over time

## Design Decisions
- Flink for stateful streaming and event-time correctness
- Raw JSON preserved in Kafka
- JSONB in PostgreSQL — no MongoDB
- Independent Maven modules — no parent pom
- Observational language throughout — never judgemental
- Do not fetch external URLs autonomously
- If documentation needed, ask user to provide the section

## Current State
Planning complete. No code written yet.
First task: exploratory adsb-collector — connect to OpenSky,
poll /states/all, print raw responses to console, understand
field population and nullability before wiring Kafka.

## Kafka Retention
30-day local retention on Redpanda. Deliberate architectural
decision — enables replaying historical events through
different algorithm versions during development, without
waiting for live recurrence of interesting events
(e.g. tanker disappearances, route anomalies).

## Adaptive Polling Strategy
adsb-collector polling is two-tier and dynamic, not fixed-interval:

Tier 1 — Discovery sweep:
  Global /states/all query every 10-15 minutes
  Identifies candidate aircraft of interest
  Adds candidates to watchlist

Tier 2 — Watchlist tracking:
  Higher frequency queries for flagged aircraft
  Interval tightens dynamically on events:
    - Tanker airborne → add to watchlist, increase frequency
    - Unusual route detected → add to watchlist
    - Aircraft disappears → maximum frequency on last known area
    - Aircraft reappears → capture precisely, then relax frequency

Collector reads watchlist from PostgreSQL or a dedicated Kafka
topic and adjusts query parameters accordingly.

Credit budget target:
  ~400 credits/day for discovery sweep (global, every 10-15 min)
  ~3,600 credits/day available for watchlist tracking

## Aircraft Metadata Reference Table
PostgreSQL table mapping icao24 → aircraft type, registration,
operator, manufacturer. Write-once, rarely-update reference data
(physical airframe attributes do not change).

Sources, in order:
  1. FAA registry CSV (free bulk download, daily updated)
  2. OpenSky aircraft database CSV (free download)
  3. planespotters.net API (free tier, for gaps only,
     result cached permanently — never re-queried)

Reload FAA + OpenSky CSVs periodically (weekly) via script
in reference-data/. This enrichment consumes zero OpenSky
API credits — credits reserved entirely for /states/all
polling.